From u5900eadecec2d82d at groups.google.com  Sat Dec  8 22:53:11 2007
From: u5900eadecec2d82d at groups.google.com (Distance Development)
Date: Sat, 08 Dec 2007 22:53:11 +0000
Subject: Re: Line transect/effort questions
Message-ID: <WKsO4o48c-w.MVSEsE3olV0J@distance-sampling.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <WKsO4o48c-w.47-TmIS7gVYJ@distance-sampling.groups.google.com>
References: <WKsO4o48c-w.47-TmIS7gVYJ@distance-sampling.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/distance-sampling/c/WKsO4o48c-w/m/MVSEsE3olV0J>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

>> >> 2. Am I correct that Distance cannot do model averaging across
>> >> covariates that I add such as burn year?
>> >> I've had some models within two AIC units of each other and would like
>> >> to do some model averaging within Distance rather than in another
>> >> program or by hand.

Steve is quite right that you can't do this automatically in Distance:
even if you select multiple key function and adjustment terms in MCDS
engines, all of these models will share the same covariates.  As Steve
suggested, doing model averaging outside of Distance, e.g., using AIC
weights is a good option.  You could also write a bootstrap routine in
some other program (e.g., R) and get it to call MCDS.exe (the program
that implements the MCDS engine) once for each covariate combination you
are interested in, for each replicate dataset.  Eric Rexstad wrote a
nice tech report showing an example of how to do this in R - you can get
it from
http://eprints.st-andrews.ac.uk/archive/00000447/
There's also some visual basic code that may be useful in the support
page of the Distance web site, and some info on calling mcds.exe in an
appendix to the online manual.

Cheers,

  - Len Thomas

